cppExamples:
class Config {
  public:
      static Config& instance() {
          static Config inst; // Initialized on FIRST call, thread-safe since C++11!
          return inst;
      }
  };

questions.answer:
If global/static object `A` in `file1.cpp` depends on global/static object `B` in `file2.cpp` during startup before `main()`, C++ gives **no guarantee** which `.cpp` file initializes first! `A` might read uninitialized `B` -> disaster (**SIOF**).  
  **Fix:** Wrap `B` inside a `static` function as a **function-local `static`**:
  ```cpp
  class Config {
  public:
      static Config& instance() {
          static Config inst; // Initialized on FIRST call, thread-safe since C++11!
          return inst;
      }
  };
  ```
  Whenever `A` calls `Config::instance()`, `inst` is guaranteed to be constructed on the spot!

