class A { public: int id; A(int x) : id(x) {} };
  class B : virtual public A { public: B() : A(1) {} };
  class C : virtual public A { public: C() : A(2) {} };
  class D : public B, public C { public: D() : A(99), B(), C() {} };

questions.answer:
Make `B` and `C` inherit `virtual`ly from `A`:
  ```cpp
  class A { public: int id; A(int x) : id(x) {} };
  class B : virtual public A { public: B() : A(1) {} };
  class C : virtual public A { public: C() : A(2) {} };
  class D : public B, public C { public: D() : A(99), B(), C() {} };
  ```
  Now `D` contains **only ONE shared copy of `A`**, so `d.id` is unambiguous (`99`)!  
  **Crucial Rule:** The **most-derived class (`D`)** is responsible for calling `A`'s constructor directly! The `A(1)` and `A(2)` calls written inside `B` and `C` are **completely ignored** when constructing a `D` object!

cppExamples:
const int& operator[](std::size_t idx) const {
      // bounds check + lookup logic
      return data_[idx];
  }
  int& operator[](std::size_t idx) {
      return const_cast<int&>(std::as_const(*this)[idx]); // C++17 std::as_const!
  }

questions.answer:
Never duplicate 30 lines of bounds-checking logic in both `T& operator[](size_t)` and `const T& operator[](size_t) const`. Instead, implement the **`const`** version cleanly, and have the **non-`const`** version delegate to it safely:
  ```cpp
  const int& operator[](std::size_t idx) const {
      // bounds check + lookup logic
      return data_[idx];
  }
  int& operator[](std::size_t idx) {
      return const_cast<int&>(std::as_const(*this)[idx]); // C++17 std::as_const!
  }
  ```
  Why is `const_cast` safe here? Because the non-`const` overload can only be called on a **non-`const` object**, so casting away `const` on the returned reference is guaranteed safe (no UB)! Never do it in the reverse direction!

cppExamples:
Buffer& operator=(Buffer other) noexcept { // Copy OR Move happens automatically!
      swap(*this, other);
      return *this;
  } // Old resources in 'other' are cleaned up here by ~Buffer()!

questions.answer:
If a class implements a non-throwing `swap(T& a, T& b) noexcept`, you can write a single unified assignment operator taking its parameter **by value**:
  ```cpp
  Buffer& operator=(Buffer other) noexcept { // Copy OR Move happens automatically!
      swap(*this, other);
      return *this;
  } // Old resources in 'other' are cleaned up here by ~Buffer()!
  ```
  It automatically handles **self-assignment**, gives **strong exception safety** (if copying `other` throws, `*this` is untouched), and covers **both** copy assignment and move assignment with zero code duplication!
